{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser stops asserting two relations the fold never gave it. Exact clean pushed head c5bd47a5a2f49b62be0f51806f7108d9a7277ed8 on request/lifecycle-none-rendering; tree 70c3bcca, checkout clean, origin matching. Base: merge-base with main is 934ebcfb, which is also current main, both verified at filing. Under planner's request 27a145f1 and my promise 9fbe12d8.\n\nTHE HYPOTHESIS I GAVE WAS WRONG AND WOULD HAVE CAUSED A REGRESSION. I read both symptoms as one root — lifecycle-none kinds getting affordances they should not — and briefed it that way. propose is lifecycle none. It is the canonical ratifiable kind and the whole reason the ratification queue exists, and I verified that against the live projection rather than taking it on report. A fix keyed on my theory would have deleted proposal ratification. The unifying story was false for both defects and they turned out to have no shared code, no shared predicate and no shared cause.\n\nSYMPTOM ONE IS ABOUT POSITION, NOT KIND. The founding seed at sequence 1 is refused ratification by the fold from every possible signer, permanently and in every workroom: decideRatify refuses an authority grant ratified by its beneficiary, and decideState admits event 0 only if it self-seeds the operator roster, so the seed is its own beneficiary by construction. decideSupersede refuses to retire it as well. So the queue was offering an act nobody can perform. isFoundingSeed keys on sequence 1, which is the fold's own record.index == 0.\n\nTHE FIX IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. Six of this workroom's seven roster records carry a real ratification the fold accepted, so excluding roster by kind would have invented a refusal — the mirror of the defect. The pre-existing test asserting that a roster row is counted still passes and serves as the positive control.\n\nSYMPTOM TWO IS A CITATION READ AS CONTAINMENT. There is no re-parenting fallback; the orphan handling in interaction.ts already does the honest thing. buildThreadIndex took the first provenance entry as the conversational parent unconditionally, so a request that opens by citing the artifact it wants reviewed made its entire commitment a descendant of somebody else's work, and then of whatever that rested on. Measured on this log, the seed's thread held 3040 of 13800 records and swallowed 431 of 1929 commitments. The walk now stops where the fold already draws the line: a record the projection places inside a commitment is not a reply to anything outside it. 1916 edges cut; the seed's thread went from 3040 records to 62. Nothing is re-parented — the records appear in their own thread, which is where a click on them already went, so two functions had been contradicting each other about the same record.\n\nONE CORRECTION TO THE REQUEST'S OWN EVIDENCE. It says nothing rests on the genesis record. Thirteen records do, proposal #6 among them. I confirmed that against provenance after first getting zero from a query against rests_on, which this projection never populates — 0 of 8955 statements carry it. My first measurement was of nothing, and it agreed with the request, which is the most dangerous way to be wrong.\n\nSYMPTOM 1b DID NOT REPRODUCE. The withdraw affordance on the seed is already closed by merge c679fb73, at both the toolbar and the signing boundary; semanticActions on that record returns an empty list. The resident on :7777 is built at that revision, so the observation predates its restart. Nothing was changed for this half and no test added, because the tests from that merge already pin it.\n\nTHE TESTS ASSERT AGAINST THE REAL PROJECTION, and each proves it rendered before asserting an absence — the expanders are collapsed by default, so a naive \"the text is absent\" assertion would have passed before the fix as well. Mutation: disabling the commitment boundary fails only the two threading tests; disabling the founding-seed predicate fails only the two ratification tests. They are independent, which is the evidence that the causes are.\n\nTWO THINGS FOUND ALONG THE WAY. A pre-existing fixture stamped sequence 1 on every record, a log no fold could produce and one that collides with the seed's position; it is renumbered and its five existing assertions are unchanged. And the resident serves the old bundle until it is restarted on a build of this head.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. npm test 135 pass, 0 fail — 131 before, four added. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet and git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"c5bd47a5a2f49b62be0f51806f7108d9a7277ed8","path":"ui"}}